body:
If the goal here is to just remove the setTimeout which closes the popover, then we should just add a close button and maybe make it popover=auto instead of popover=manual.

I previously suggested using popovertarget, but if the author code does a bunch of stuff to determine whether the result is successful or not, perhaps including a request to a server, then I don't think we want to synchronously show a popover.

How about this?

```html
<button id=submit>Submit</button>
<p><output><span popover=auto id=outspan></span></output></p>

<script>
  const sBtn = document.getElementById("submit");
  const outSpan = document.getElementById("outspan");

  sBtn.addEventListener("click", async () => {
    let successMessage;
    let errorMessage;
    let success;

    /* Define logic for determining success of an action
       and determining the appropriate success or error
       messages to use... */

    if (success) {
      outSpan.textContent = successMessage;
    } else {
      outSpan.textContent = errorMessage;
    }

    outSpan.showPopover();
 });
</script>
```

@keithamus How about hover use-case ?

- pointerover: showPopover after 500ms delay
- pointerleave: hidePopover after 200ms delay

it's inspired by [MUI tooltip](https://mui.com/material-ui/react-tooltip/#showing-and-hiding).
it uses `setTimeout` but not "Time Limit UI" which WCAG mentions I believe.
seems valid use-cases to use JS show/hide for me.

```js
$div.addEventListener("mouseover", () => {
  setTimeout(() => {
    $tooltip.showPopover()
  }, 500)
})

$div.addEventListener("mouseout", () => {
  setTimeout(() => {
    $tooltip.hidePopover()
  }, 200)
})
```

